{"subjectId":"c","id":"recursion","title":"Recursion","group":"Control Flow & Functions","order":16,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A recursive function calls itself on a smaller problem until it reaches a <em>base case</em>. Each call gets its own stack frame, so depth is limited by the stack size.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Anatomy</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nunsigned long long factorial(unsigned n) {\n    if (n &lt;= 1) return 1;              /* base case */\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);       /* recursive case: smaller input */\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    for (unsigned i = 0; i &lt;= 5; i++) printf(\"%u! = %llu\\n\", i, factorial(i));\n    printf(\"20! = %llu\\n\", factorial(20));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0! = 1\n1! = 1\n2! = 2\n3! = 6\n4! = 24\n5! = 120\n20! = 2432902008176640000</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>How the call stack unwinds</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid count(int n, int depth) {\n    if (n == 0) { printf(\"%*sbase\\n\", depth * 2, \"\"); return; }\n    printf(\"%*senter %d\\n\", depth * 2, \"\", n);\n    count(n - 1, depth + 1);\n    printf(\"%*sleave %d\\n\", depth * 2, \"\", n);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    count(3, 0);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>enter 3\n  enter 2\n    enter 1\n      base\n    leave 1\n  leave 2\nleave 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tree\"></i>Classic recursive functions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint gcd(int a, int b) { return b == 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b); }   /* Euclid, tail-recursive */\n\nlong power(long base, int exp) {                                /* fast exponentiation O(log n) */\n    if (exp == 0) return 1;\n    long half = power(base, exp / 2);\n    return exp % 2 ? half * half * base : half * half;\n}\n\nint sum_digits(int n) { return n &lt; 10 ? n : n % 10 + sum_digits(n / 10); }\n\nvoid hanoi(int n, char from, char to, char via) {\n    if (n == 0) return;\n    hanoi(n - 1, from, via, to);\n    printf(\"move disk %d %c-&gt;%c\\n\", n, from, to);\n    hanoi(n - 1, via, to, from);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"gcd=%d pow=%ld digits=%d\\n\", gcd(84, 36), power(3, 13), sum_digits(98765));\n    hanoi(2, 'A', 'C', 'B');\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>gcd=12 pow=1594323 digits=35\nmove disk 1 A-&gt;B\nmove disk 2 A-&gt;C\nmove disk 1 B-&gt;C</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-line\"></i>Exponential blow-up and memoisation</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nstatic long calls = 0;\n\nlong fib_naive(int n) {\n    calls++;\n    return n &lt; 2 ? n : fib_naive(n - 1) + fib_naive(n - 2);\n}\n\nlong memo[91];\nlong fib_memo(int n) {\n    if (n &lt; 2) return n;\n    if (memo[n]) return memo[n];\n    return memo[n] = fib_memo(n - 1) + fib_memo(n - 2);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"fib(25) = %ld using %ld calls\\n\", fib_naive(25), calls);\n    printf(\"fib(90) = %ld\\n\", fib_memo(90));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>fib(25) = 75025 using 242785 calls\nfib(90) = 2880067194370816120</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Recursion</th><th>Iteration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Natural for trees, divide &amp; conquer, backtracking</td><td>Natural for linear scans</td></tr><tr><td>Uses O(depth) stack — risk of <strong>stack overflow</strong></td><td>O(1) extra space</td></tr><tr><td>Function-call overhead</td><td>No call overhead</td></tr><tr><td>Tail calls <em>may</em> be optimised at <code>-O2</code> (not guaranteed by C)</td><td>Always constant stack</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Missing or unreachable base case → infinite recursion → <em>stack overflow</em> (segfault). Default stacks are only ~8 MB on Linux/macOS main threads, often much less on threads and embedded targets.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is tail recursion?</summary><div><p>A call is a <em>tail call</em> when it is the last action of the function (<code>return f(n - 1, acc * n);</code>). The compiler can then reuse the current stack frame, turning recursion into a loop. C compilers do this at higher optimisation levels, but the standard does not guarantee it.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the time complexity of naive recursive Fibonacci?</summary><div><p>O(φⁿ) ≈ O(1.618ⁿ) calls because every call branches into two overlapping subproblems. Memoisation or a bottom-up loop makes it O(n).</p></div></details></section>"}